      "The 'Absence of Clamping Clause' / RAW Argument (advanced by Colleagues 3, 4, 8, 10): Hostettler's text contains no explicit min/max clamping clause. In card games, mathematical boundaries are never assumed without explicit text.",
      "The Invariant and Shortcut Identity: 125 + (-25) = 100 and 100 - (-25) = 125 hold algebraically and bidirectionally.",
      "Digital Engine Benchmark: BGA, BrettspielWelt, and Yucata (licensed platforms) explicitly score the hand as +125 and -25."
    ],
    "our_counter_strategy_and_rebuttals": [
      "Reframe 'Confiscation' via Closed Deck Economy: In point-trick games, card face values are internal weights determining a team's fractional share of the deal's finite 100-point bounty, not independent sovereign currency. Team A didn't have 25 points stolen; rather, the deck never possessed 125 net points to award. The Phoenix's -25 balances the Dragon's +25 at the deck level. Taking 100% of positive cards captures 100% of the deck's bounty: 100 points.",
      "Reframe 'Absence of Floor Clause' via Game Taxonomy: In trick-taking taxonomy, trick collection is fundamentally an inventory-acquisition mechanic with a structural default boundary at zero. In contrast, negative scoring is an extraordinary exception that must be explicitly codified. Hostettler explicitly codified negative scoring for failed bids (-100, -200), but deliberately did not do so for trick capture.",
      "Dismantle Digital Engines as Naive Parsers: Digital platforms run naive `sum(cards)` scripts over an array. Software parsing artifacts on rare edge cases do not reflect tabletop game jurisprudence or designer intent."
    ]
  },
